    ":question/problem": "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}\n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-4\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4t}{{t}^{2}+1})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-4\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({t}^{2}+3)^{2})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}}\\right)+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
